Backport of GHSA-h25m-26qc-wcjf / CVE-2026-23864 for next 14.2.21.

Next.js HTTP request deserialization can lead to DoS when using React Server
Components (CWE-400 / CWE-502). The bundled react-server-dom-* server runtime
deserializes a BigInt from an untrusted RSC / Server-Action request body via
BigInt(value.slice(2)) with no bound on the number of digits. A crafted request
carrying a very long numeric literal forces an unbounded, super-linear BigInt
allocation/parse that exhausts CPU/memory (denial of service).

Next.js published no official 14.x fix; the earliest official fix is 15.0.8,
which (together with the React RSC backports 19.0.4/19.1.5/19.2.4) adds a
MAX_BIGINT_DIGITS resource limit that throws "BigInt is too large" before the
value is materialized. This patch applies the same targeted guard (300-digit
ceiling) to every runtime-reachable bundled RSC server variant in 14.2.21
(react-server-dom-webpack and -turbopack, node and edge, development and
production.min), matching the approach of the existing internal 14.2.2 and
14.2.35 backports. A regression test is added.

